366:A dynamic skills file resolves which [skill](../skills) a caller can load, keyed on the principal. It resolves on `session.started` and `turn.started` only (`step.started` is reserved for dynamic tools). Read `ctx.session.auth` or channel metadata and return a `defineSkill(...)` (named after the file slug) or `null`:
385:Skills follow the same naming rule as tools: a single `defineSkill(...)` is named after the file slug, while a map names each entry by its bare key (namespace the key yourself if it might collide). A dynamic skill overrides a same-named authored one; two dynamic resolvers emitting the same name throws.
387:## Dynamic instructions
389:A dynamic instructions file returns `defineInstructions({ content, role? })` built from the principal, tenant, channel, or external data. Omit `role` for system context:
391:```ts title="agent/instructions/persona.ts"
392:import { defineDynamic, defineInstructions } from "eve/instructions";
408:```ts title="agent/instructions/brief.ts"
409:import { defineDynamic, defineInstructions } from "eve/instructions";
414:    "turn.started": async (_event, ctx) => {
422:Instruction resolvers support `session.started` and `turn.started` only. A system result lives in that scope and stays outside history. A user result is appended to history at the lifecycle boundary, with session results before turn results and both before the current delivery. There is no automatic deduplication: returning the same user content on a later turn intentionally appends another message.
424:Resolver snapshots reflect that order. At `session.started`, `ctx.messages` includes static user-role instructions. At `turn.started`, it also includes user-role results from `session.started`. These augmented snapshots are specific to instruction resolvers; tools, skills, models, and subagents keep their existing message snapshots.
426:Returning `null` or blank content contributes nothing. A throwing or invalid session resolver leaves any wider valid system selection in place. Every turn starts with fresh turn-scoped system instructions, so a failed or empty turn result cannot leak the previous turn's value. Completed lifecycle steps are replay-safe: parking, resuming, or replaying them does not duplicate user-role messages.
   390	
   391	```ts title="agent/instructions/persona.ts"
   392	import { defineDynamic, defineInstructions } from "eve/instructions";
   393	
   394	export default defineDynamic({
   395	  events: {
   396	    "session.started": (_event, ctx) => {
   397	      const plan = ctx.session.auth.current?.attributes.plan ?? "free";
   398	      return defineInstructions({
   399	        content: `The caller is on the ${plan} plan. Match the depth of your answers to it.`,
   400	      });
   401	    },
   402	  },
   403	});
   404	```
   405	
   406	Use `role: "user"` when the resolved value is application or user context that should become part of durable history:
   407	
   408	```ts title="agent/instructions/brief.ts"
   409	import { defineDynamic, defineInstructions } from "eve/instructions";
   410	import { loadBrief } from "../lib/briefs";
   411	
   412	export default defineDynamic({
   413	  events: {
   414	    "turn.started": async (_event, ctx) => {
   415	      const brief = await loadBrief(ctx.session.auth.current);
   416	      return brief ? defineInstructions({ content: brief, role: "user" }) : null;
   417	    },
   418	  },
   419	});
   420	```
   421	
   422	Instruction resolvers support `session.started` and `turn.started` only. A system result lives in that scope and stays outside history. A user result is appended to history at the lifecycle boundary, with session results before turn results and both before the current delivery. There is no automatic deduplication: returning the same user content on a later turn intentionally appends another message.
   423	
   424	Resolver snapshots reflect that order. At `session.started`, `ctx.messages` includes static user-role instructions. At `turn.started`, it also includes user-role results from `session.started`. These augmented snapshots are specific to instruction resolvers; tools, skills, models, and subagents keep their existing message snapshots.
   425	
   426	Returning `null` or blank content contributes nothing. A throwing or invalid session resolver leaves any wider valid system selection in place. Every turn starts with fresh turn-scoped system instructions, so a failed or empty turn result cannot leak the previous turn's value. Completed lifecycle steps are replay-safe: parking, resuming, or replaying them does not duplicate user-role messages.
   427	
   428	Dynamic system content that changes frequently can reduce provider prompt-cache reuse. Prefer session scope for stable values and use turn scope only when the context must be refreshed. Cache behavior remains provider-specific.
